<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raza Ali Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raza Ali Khan was the last ruling Nawab of Rampur, a Muslim princely state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a>, reigning from 1930. He introduced administrative reforms and expanded the state's famous library of Persian and Arabic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. He acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947, and Rampur was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in 1949. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raza-library">Raza Library</a> at Rampur takes its name from his family.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Raza Ali Khan was born on 17 November 1908 and was styled Nawabzada and Wali Ahad, the heir, until he succeeded as Nawab of Rampur in 1930, when his style took in the titles Ali Jah, Mukhlis ud-Daula, Nasir ul-Mulk, Amir ul-Umara and Mustaid Jang. Described as a tolerant and progressive ruler, he brought more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> into his government, among them his Prime Minister, Lt. Col. Horilal Varma, expanded the irrigation system, completed electrification projects and went on building schools, roads and sewage systems.</p><p>He sent his soldiers to fight in the Middle Eastern theatres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. His style recorded military ranks rising from Lieutenant in 1932 to Major-General in 1946, and he was made KCSI in 1936 and GCIE in 1944, besides holding the Nishan-i-Hamidia and Nishan-i-Iqbal of his own house, the King George V Silver Jubilee Medal of 1935 and the Indian Independence Medal of 1947. Rampur acceded to the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on 15 August 1947 and was later merged into it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the merger he devoted himself to charitable projects and to the Freemasons, becoming the first Grand Master of the Grand Lodge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a title his style carried from 1961. He wrote and translated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> on his princely state, religion and administration, and his poems and songs in Braj Bhasha about the festival of Holi are still sung in the region. He died on 6 March 1966, aged 57, and like his father was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. His eldest son, Murtaza Ali Khan Bahadur, succeeded him as head of the family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raza Ali Khan of Rampur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Raza Ali Khan</b> acceded to India, and Rampur was merged into Uttar Pradesh in 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/rampur-state">Rampur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rampur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Rampur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.79,79.02&amp;year=1945" aria-label="Rampur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Raza Ali Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4182 5064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4182 5064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.79,79.02&amp;year=1945">What was here in 1945? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
